What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/
Date:		October, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.5
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		One directory per instance of a CXL Hot-range Monitoring Unit
		(CHMU, CXL r4.0 section 8.2.8): block Y of memdev X, instance Z.
		An instance counts accesses to units of the device's memory and
		reports the units whose count crossed a threshold in a hotlist.
		It has one user at a time, whichever takes it first: see
		owner.

		With CONFIG_CXL_HMU_DAMON the name of this directory,
		hmu_memX.Y.Z, is also the name of a DAMOS hot range source:
		write it to the "source" file of a migrate_hot or migrate_cold
		scheme of a DAMON physical address space context (see
		Documentation/ABI/testing/sysfs-kernel-mm-damon) to have the
		pages of the units the instance reports migrated to the
		scheme's target node, under the scheme's quota and filters.
		The worker thread of the DAMON context then takes the
		instance, starts it with the configuration staged in the files
		below and no interrupt, and polls it: at each apply interval
		of the scheme, and while the scheme's quota has room, it takes
		batches of 32 entries off the hotlist, translates each unit to
		host physical ranges and hands them to the scheme, the unit
		with the highest count of a batch first if the instance runs
		in epoch mode. Entries it does not take stay in the hotlist,
		so a quota that is used up shows as overflows growing, not as
		work queued in the kernel. The entries of a batch that could
		not be handed over, because the scheme refused a range,
		because a unit could not be translated without waiting for a
		lock of the memory device or because a unit has no System
		RAM behind it (no region maps it, or a device-dax one does),
		are counted in dropped; the device reports a unit again
		while it stays hot. The instance is
		stopped and given up when the scheme stops naming it, when
		its DAMON worker thread stops, and at unbind.

		The scheme's "source_state" file tells whether the instance
		was taken; "error <errno>" there is, for this source: -16 if
		the instance is held (a perf event is open on it, or its
		range overlaps that of a held sibling and Address Overlap is
		not supported) or a lock of the memory device was not free;
		-22 for a unit_size above the size of a PMD mapping (2 MiB on
		x86-64; such a unit is for perf) or a hotness_threshold that
		does not fit the counter; -6 if no committed region maps the
		instance's range or the node of that memory has no online
		memory; -19 while the cxl_mem driver is not bound to the
		memory device; -95 if the only regions that map the range
		use an addressing that cannot be translated to host physical
		addresses; -5 or -110 if the instance cannot be programmed.
		DAMON tries again at every apply interval of the scheme, so
		the source is taken as soon as what prevented it is gone.
		After a -5 or a -110 the instance is left alone for 10
		seconds: it is not taken and its registers are not written,
		and the same error is shown until the next real attempt.


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/owner
Date:		November, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.5
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RO) Who holds the instance: "perf" while a perf event is open
		on the instance's PMU (CONFIG_CXL_HMU_PERF), from
		perf_event_open() until the event is closed, whether or not
		it is enabled; "damon" while a DAMOS scheme has the instance
		started as its hot range source (CONFIG_CXL_HMU_DAMON); else
		"none". There is nothing to write: an instance is taken by
		using it, first come, first served. Whoever comes second
		does not get it: perf_event_open() fails with -EBUSY, and a
		DAMOS scheme shows "error -16" in its source_state file and
		takes the instance once it is free. An instance cannot be
		reserved for a user that is not using it. A scheme that
		tries and fails to start the instance holds it for the
		length of the attempt.


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/unit_size
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/hotness_threshold
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/epoch_us
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/range_base
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/range_size
Date:		November, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.5
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RW) The configuration an in-kernel user (a DAMOS scheme that
		names the instance as its source) starts the instance with.
		Only a write to these files changes them. A
		perf event is configured by its own attributes and neither
		reads nor changes them. A store takes effect at the next
		start by an in-kernel user; one that is running keeps what it
		started with. Each value is checked against the instance's
		capabilities at store time: a value the instance does not
		support is refused with -EOPNOTSUPP, an out-of-range one with
		-EINVAL or -ERANGE, and a refused store leaves the old value.
		Where a file has the name of a parameter of the instance's
		perf PMU (hotness_threshold, range_base, range_size) it has
		the same meaning and unit.

		unit_size: bytes per counted unit, a power of two from
		supported_unit_sizes. The default is 4096 if supported, else
		the smallest. (The PMU's hotness_granual is its log2.)

		hotness_threshold: the count at which a unit is reported,
		default 1. It must be below 1 << (the counter width), which
		the device only reports once the unit size is programmed, so
		a value that is too large is refused with -EINVAL by the
		start, not by the store; with dynamic debug enabled for the
		driver the kernel log names the width.

		epoch_us: the epoch length in microseconds, clamped to
		epoch_range_us and rounded down to what the device encodes
		(a multiplier of 1 to 4095 on a scale of 100 us, 1 ms, 10 ms,
		100 ms or 1 s); reads back the effective value. The default
		is one second. 0 is -EINVAL; a length the device cannot
		encode is -ERANGE.

		range_base, range_size: the device physical address range
		counted, in units of 256 MiB; base + size must not exceed
		capacity_ranges, size must not be 0 and the range must hold at
		least one unit. The default is the whole device.

		What has no file is fixed for an in-kernel user: epoch mode
		if the instance supports it, else always-on; reads and writes
		of any kind if supported, else non-TEE reads and writes, else
		the first access type the instance offers; the smallest
		down-sampling factor, not randomized.


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/supported_modes
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/supported_unit_sizes
