What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/hotlist_size
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/capacity_ranges
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/address_overlap
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/postponed_overflow
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/interrupts
Date:		November, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.5
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RO) What the instance advertises: what a perf event or the
		files above may ask of it, and how to read what it reports.

		supported_modes: "epoch" (a unit is reported when its count
		crosses the threshold within an epoch, and the count is
		reported with it) and/or "always_on" (a unit is reported once
		its count crosses the threshold; the reported count is not
		valid), space separated. They are the PMU's epoch_type 0 and
		1.

		supported_unit_sizes: in bytes, space separated.

		supported_access_types: the requests that can be counted,
		space separated: "nontee_r", "nontee_w", "nontee_rw", "r",
		"w", "rw", which are the PMU's access_type 1 to 6.

		supported_downsampling: the factors, space separated; the
		PMU's downsampling_factor is the log2 of one of them.

		epoch_range_us: "min max", the shortest and the longest epoch
		in microseconds.

		epoch_scope: "global" if every counter shares one epoch,
		"per_counter" if each has its own.

		hotlist_size: in entries.

		capacity_ranges: the device capacity in 256 MiB units, the
		bound of range_base + range_size.

		address_overlap: 1 if this instance may count a range that
		overlaps a sibling instance's. Taking an instance whose range
		overlaps that of a sibling that is held, running or not,
		fails with -EBUSY unless both instances have it.

		postponed_overflow: 1 if hot units survive a hotlist overflow,
		so that an overflow delays them and loses none.

		interrupts: "overflow" and/or "level", space separated, or
		empty. The PMU exists only for an instance with "level" and
		a usable interrupt vector; an in-kernel user polls and needs
		neither.


What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/active
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/overflows
What:		/sys/bus/cxl/devices/hmu_memX.Y/hmu_memX.Y.Z/dropped
Date:		November, 2026
KernelVersion:	v7.5
Contact:	linux-cxl@vger.kernel.org
Description:
		(RO) State. active is 1 while the instance is enabled; an
		instance can be held (see owner) without being enabled, as by
		a perf event that is open but disabled. overflows and dropped
		are the two ways a hot unit is lost, since the last start.
		overflows counts the hotlist overflows the device reported:
		the list was full when a unit became hot, and unless
		postponed_overflow is 1 that unit is gone. dropped counts the
		hotlist entries the kernel read and threw away: for a perf
		event, those its AUX buffer had no room for when it stopped;
		for a DAMOS scheme, those of a batch that could not be handed
		over to it, the ones without System RAM behind them
		included.
